(6-b, b-1). To maximize, set b such that 6-b = b-1 => b = 3.5, not integer. For b=3, min(6-3=3, 3-1=2) = 2 => 2 points. For b=4, min(6-4=2, 4-1=3) = 2 => 2 points. So max 2 points, less than bound 3. So lines with |p+q|=1 cannot achieve bound for k=5. So bound holds.\n\nThus bound (2.5) seems correct.\n\nNow the solution's argument that only three lines achieve bound for odd k: they claim lines with |p+q|=2 give three possibilities: p=q=1 (slope 1), p=-2,q=1 (slope -2), p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2). But we saw p=-2,q=1 gives p+q=-1, not 2. So there is a mistake: they mis-identified lines with |p+q|=2. Actually lines with |p+q|=2 are those where p+q = ±2. Solutions: p+q=2 => p = 2 - q. For q=1 => p=1 => p=q=1 (slope 1). For q=2 => p=0 => horizontal line (not sunny). For q=3 => p=-1 => p=-1, q=3 => slope -1/3 (sunny). For q=4 => p=-2 => slope -0.5? Actually p=-2, q=4 => slope -0.5, but gcd(p,q)=2, not allowed (must be gcd=1). So need gcd(p,q)=1. So p=-1,q=3 is allowed. That line has slope -1/3. Does it achieve bound? Let's test for k=5: slope -1/3 line y = -x/3 + b. For integer points, x must be multiple of 3. So points (3t, -t + b). For T_5, x=3t ≤5 => t ≤1. So only t=1 possible (x=3). Then y = -1 + b. Need y≥1 => b≥2. Also x+y ≤6 => 3 + (-t + b) ≤6 => 3 -1 + b ≤6 => b ≤4. So b ∈ {2,3,4}. For each b, we get one point: (3, b-1). So at most 1 point. So not achieving bound.\n\nThus only lines with p+q=2 (i.e., p=q=1) and p+q=-2 (i.e., p = -2 - q) produce bound? Let's examine p+q=-2 => p = -2 - q. For q=1 => p=-3 => slope -3. For q=2 => p=-4 => gcd(4,2)=2 not allowed. For q=3 => p=-5 => gcd(5,3)=1, slope -5/3. Does slope -3 line achieve bound? For k=5, line y = -3x + b. For integer points, x must be small because y must be positive. For x=1 => y = -3 + b => need y≥1 => b≥4. Also x+y ≤6 => 1 + (-3 + b) ≤6 => b ≤8. So b ∈ [4,8]. For each b, we get point (1, b-3). That's up to 5 points? Actually b can be 4,5,6,7,8 => 5 points. But need also x+y ≤6: For b=8, y=5 => sum=6 => okay. So line y = -3x + b can have up to 5 points? Let's test b=8: points (1,5) sum=6; (2,2) sum=4; (3,-1) invalid. So only x=1 and x=2? Wait need to check integer points for x=2: y = -6 + b => for b=8 => y=2 => sum=4, okay. So (2,2) also works. For b=8, we have (1,5) and (2,2). For b=7: (1,4) sum=5, (2,1) sum=3. So 2 points. For b=6: (1,3) sum=4, (2,0) invalid. So 1 point. So max 2 points. So slope -3 lines also achieve at most 2 points for k=5. So bound holds.\n\nThus only lines with slope 1, -2, -1/2 achieve bound for odd k? Let's test slope -2 line: y = -2x + b. For k=5, we saw b=7 gave 3 points. So slope -2 can achieve bound. Slope -1/2 also can achieve bound? For k=5, we saw max 2 points. Actually slope -1/2 gave max 2 points for k=5. So maybe only slope 1 and -2 achieve bound for odd k? But they claim also -1/2. Let's test for k=3: slope -1/2 line y = -0.5 x + b. For integer points, x even. For k=3, T_3 points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). For slope -1/2, possible points: x=2 => y = -1 + b => need y integer. For b=2 => y=1 => point (2,1). For b=3 => y=2 => (2,2). For b=4 => y=3 => (2,3) sum=5 >4? Actually k=3 => x+y ≤4. (2,3) sum=5 >4, not allowed. So only (2,1) and (2,2). That's 2 points. So slope -1/2 also gives 2 points for k=3, which matches bound floor((k+1)/2) = floor(4/2)=2. So slope -1/2 works for k=3. For k=5, slope -1/2 gave max 2 points, not 3. So maybe for larger odd k, slope -1/2 cannot achieve bound. Let's test k=7: floor((k+1)/2) = floor(8/2)=4. Does slope -1/2 achieve 4 points? Points (2t, -t + b). Need 2t + (-t + b) ≤ 8 => t + b ≤ 8 => t ≤ 8 - b. Also y ≥ 1 => -t + b ≥ 1 => t ≤ b - 1. So t ≤ min(8 - b, b - 1). Max t occurs when b ~ (8+1)/2 = 4.5. For integer b=4 or 5. For b=4: min(8-4=4, 4-1=3) = 3 => t ∈ [1,3] => 3 points. For b=5: min(8-5=3,5-1=4) = 3 => t ∈ [1,3] => 3 points. So max 3 points, less than bound 4. So slope -1/2 fails for k≥5. So only slopes 1 and -2 achieve bound for all odd k? Let's test slope -2 for k=7: floor((k+1)/2)=4. 